$$
\text{Distance} = \frac{|\vec{v} \cdot (\vec{a_2} - \vec{a_1})|}{|\vec{v}|}
$$

where:
- $\vec{v}$ is the direction vector of the lines,
- $\vec{a_1}$ and $\vec{a_2}$ are points on each line,
- The cross product of the direction vector and the vector between a point on one line and a point on the other line is used to compute the magnitude.

First, we identify the direction vectors of the lines. The direction vector of the first line is $\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -9 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}$, and the direction vector of the second line is $\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -18 \\ -4 \end{pmatrix}$. Since the lines are parallel, these direction vectors are scalar multiples of each other. Indeed, $\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -18 \\ -4 \end{pmatrix} = 2 \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -9 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}$, so they are parallel.

